---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, socials } from '~/site';
---
<Base title="About" description={site.description} section="about">
  <article class="post-wrap">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm Ben Blaker, a systems engineer. Most of my work is software — Go and TypeScript,
        usually infrastructure-shaped: routers, operators, CLIs, and the tooling that makes
        other engineers faster. The rest is the environment that software runs in: a k3s
        cluster in my office, the network around it, and the printers that make the parts
        holding it together.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've spent most of the last decade in SRE and platform engineering roles, mostly at
        companies where the infrastructure got large enough to start hurting. That work
        teaches you a particular kind of humility — that the interesting failures are almost
        never the ones you designed for, and that the graph you didn't think to draw is the
        one you'll want at 3am.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        The through-line is that I like understanding things all the way down. It's the same
        instinct whether it's reading kernel networking code, tearing a printer apart to find
        why the first layer drifts, or working out which of six nodes is lying about its
        state. I'm suspicious of abstractions I can't see through, which is less a principle
        than a compulsion.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means I'd rather run something myself once than read three blog posts
        about it. The homelab exists because a cluster you can break at 11pm teaches you
        things a managed one never will. The printers exist for the same reason, and have the
        additional virtue of failing in ways you can hold.
      </p>

      <h2>What's here</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/projects/">Projects</a> are long-lived things with a status and a spec
        sheet. The <a href="/log/">log</a> is dated entries — build notes attached to a
        project, longer essays, and short notes. The <a href="/projects/#backlog">backlog</a>
        is public, which I find more honest than pretending the list is finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        I try to write things down before I know how they turn out. A build log written on day
        one is more useful than a polished retrospective written three weeks later, because
        the uncertainty is the interesting part and it doesn't survive being remembered. Some
        of these entries will age badly. That's the point of dating them.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <p>
        {socials.map((s, i) => (
          <Fragment>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={s.href} rel={s.rel}>{s.label}</a></Fragment>
        ))}
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
